00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
most fishermen on the banks of this  one's clean and flowing river are now  struggling to keep their livelihood  going no rahmet's family has fished in  the carnivale river for generations  there used to be an abundance of fish  here but over the years due to various  types of factory waste garbage and  polythene fish stocks are gradually  disappearing here  nowadays we barely get any fish aside  from some small shrimps it's hard for us  to manage a living anymore according to  a study bangladesh generates more than  800 000 tons of plastic waste a year  it's also reported to make up more than  60 of the leader found on the country's  beaches  and keeping the rivers clean is becoming  harder  we find various types of wastes like  polythenes plastics etc making it very  difficult for us to dredge and delays  our work  the chartergram port authority says it  recently found a layer of polythene up  to three meters thick while dredging the  riverbed  you can see thick layer of silt like  this at different points all across the  carnivale river here in chatogram pod  city this is due to years of dumping of  plastic polythene industrial and  household waste in the river  although bangladesh was the world's  first to ban plastic shopping bags due  to lack of enforcement and public  awareness polythene is still widely used  unless we manage to stop the production  usage and banning of polythene then at  some stage the kana fully river the  lifeline of chattagram will be totally  clogged the operation of the port will  be at stake  according to industry insiders there are  more than a thousand small plastic  recycling factories across the country  that produce plastic flakes from scraps  and some export them  we receive this from waste collectors  then we break them into microchips which  is later used once again to make plastic  products  but creating more plastic products isn't  helping tackle the problem  and the u.n says every day about 73 000  tons of plastic waste were entering the  bay of bengal through bangladesh some of  which comes from neighboring countries  in the aquatic ecosystem plastics  degraded into smaller sized  microplastics  that eventually enter into the food  chain  which is a big concern for food safety  and human health  environmental experts want the gradual  accumulation of pollution in the bay of  bengal may not only further threaten  fishing communities but also hamper  shipping and maritime trade chanwi  chaudhary jazeera choctawgram bangladesh
 

